<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-inverse-of-polynomials" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Inverse of Polynomials</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine the inverse of a function of polynomials and trigonometric ratios
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Guidance on the Activity</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Students experience the idea of reversing a function physically before working with algebra. By handling cards with inputs on one side and outputs on the other, they build an intuitive understanding of what an inverse function does.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Prepare a set of cards for each pair or group. Each card should show an input value of <m>f(x) = 2x + 6</m> on one side and leave the other side blank for students to calculate and write the output. After completing the forward direction, ask students to flip the cards and work in the opposite direction, starting from the output to find the original input. Prompt students by asking what operation they are undoing at each step and whether the order of undoing matters. Avoid confirming the algebraic rule until students have had time to articulate the reversal process in their own words.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->

        <activity xml:id="activity-exploring-inverse-polynomials">
        <p>
            Work in pairs for this activity. You will use a set of function cards to explore how a function transforms an input into an output and how to reverse that process to recover the original input.
        </p>
        <p>
            Each card in your set has an input value written on one side. Use the function <m>f(x) = 2x + 6</m> to calculate the output for each card and write it on the other side.
        </p>
        <table>
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="medium" right="medium">
                <row>
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>5</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>10</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            Now flip all your cards over so that the output values are facing up. Starting only from the output values, work backward to determine the original input value that produced each output.
        </p>

        <table>
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="medium" right="medium">
                <row>
                    <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>8</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>16</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>20</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            After completing both sides of all the cards, discuss the questions below with your partner.
        </p>

        <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Describe the steps you used to work backward from <m>f(x)</m> to find <m>x</m>. In what order did you reverse the operations that the function applied?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Let the output of the function be represented by the letter <m>y</m>. Write an algebraic expression in terms of <m>y</m> that gives you the original input <m>x</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Now consider the polynomial function <m>g(x) = x^2</m>. If the output is <m>25</m>, can you determine exactly one original input value? Explain your reasoning and give an example to support your answer.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Based on your observations from both functions, what characteristic must a polynomial function have in order to have an inverse function?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize the concepts and properties learners discovered in the activity.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Ask students to share the algebraic expression they wrote in question (b) of the activity. Guide them to see that what they wrote is the inverse function, and introduce the notation <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> at this point as a name for the rule they already found. Emphasize that the superscript <m>-1</m> does not mean a reciprocal; it is simply the standard way of writing the name of the inverse function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>One-to-One Functions:</alert> Use question (c) of the activity to motivate the distinction between one-to-one and many-to-one functions. Draw a quick sketch of <m>g(x) = x^2</m> on the board and show that a horizontal line at <m>y = 25</m> crosses the graph at two points. Ask students what this means for working backward. Then contrast this with the card activity, where each output came from exactly one input.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>The Four Steps:</alert> Formalize the algebraic method by working through one example on the board together, following the four steps in the insight block. Let students check each step against what they did intuitively with the cards.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

        <insight>
        <p>
            The notation <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> is read as "f inverse of x" and refers to the function that reverses what <m>f</m> does. It is important to note that the superscript <m>-1</m> here does not mean the reciprocal <m>\frac{1}{f(x)}</m>. It is simply the standard notation used to name an inverse function.
        </p>
        <p>
            A function has an inverse function only when it is <em>one-to-one</em>, meaning that each output value corresponds to exactly one input value. A function that sends two or more different inputs to the same output is called <em>many-to-one</em> and does not have an inverse over its full domain.
        </p>
        <p>
            To check whether a function is one-to-one, we can apply the <em>horizontal line test</em> to its graph. If every horizontal line crosses the graph at most once, then the function is one-to-one and has an inverse function. If any horizontal line crosses the graph at two or more points, then the function is many-to-one and does not have an inverse function over all real numbers.
        </p>
        <p>
            To determine the inverse of a polynomial function algebraically, reverse the roles of the input and output values by following these steps.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Replace the function notation <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Interchange the variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. This step reflects the fact that the inputs of the original function become the outputs of the inverse, and the outputs become the inputs.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Solve the new equation for <m>y</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Rewrite the final expression using inverse notation <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>. The resulting expression is the inverse function.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
            In contrast, polynomial functions with even powers such as <m>f(x) = x^2</m> are not one-to-one over all real numbers because different inputs can produce the same output. For instance, <m>f(2) = 4</m> and <m>f(-2) = 4</m>. Such functions do not have inverses unless their domains are restricted to make them one-to-one.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-linear">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the inverse function of the polynomial function <m>f(x) = 5x - 4</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Start by replacing <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m>.
            </p>
            <p><md>y = 5x - 4</md></p>
            <p>
                Interchange the variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> to begin reversing the function.
            </p>
            <p><md>x = 5y - 4</md></p>
            <p>
                Solve the equation for <m>y</m> by isolating the variable on one side.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                <mrow>x + 4 \amp = 5y</mrow>
                <mrow>y \amp = \frac{x + 4}{5}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>f(x)</m> is a linear function with a non-zero slope, it is one-to-one, so we can write the inverse function using the notation <m>f^{-1}(x)</m>.
            </p>
            <p><md>f^{-1}(x) = \frac{x + 4}{5}</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-exists-or-not">
        <statement>
            <p>
                For each of the following functions, use the horizontal line test to determine whether an inverse function exists. Give a reason for each answer.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                    <li><m>f(x) = x^3</m></li>
                    <li><m>g(x) = x^2 - 4</m></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The graph of <m>f(x) = x^3</m> is shown below.
                        </p>
                        <figure xml:id="fig-cubic-hlt">
                            <caption>Graph of <m>f(x) = x^3</m> passing the horizontal line test</caption>
                            <image width="55%" xml:id="img-cubic-hlt">
                                <latex-image>
                                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
                                        \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-3,-4) grid (3,4);
                                        \draw[->, thick] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                        \draw[->, thick] (0,-4.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                        \draw[ultra thick, blue, &lt;->, domain=-1.58:1.58, samples=100]
                                            plot (\x, {\x*\x*\x});
                                        \draw[dashed, red, thick] (-3,2) -- (3,2)
                                            node[right] {\small any horizontal line};
                                        \node[right] at (1.2,2.8) {\small $f(x)=x^3$};
                                    \end{tikzpicture}
                                </latex-image>
                            </image>
                        </figure>
                        <p>
                            Every horizontal line crosses the graph of <m>f(x) = x^3</m> at most once. The function is therefore one-to-one and an inverse function exists.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The graph of <m>g(x) = x^2 - 4</m> is shown below.
                        </p>
                        <figure xml:id="fig-parabola-hlt">
                            <caption>Graph of <m>g(x) = x^2 - 4</m> failing the horizontal line test</caption>
                            <image width="55%" xml:id="img-parabola-hlt">
                                <latex-image>
                                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
                                        \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-3,-5) grid (3,5);
                                        \draw[->, thick] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                        \draw[->, thick] (0,-5.5) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                        \draw[ultra thick, blue, &lt;->, domain=-2.5:2.5, samples=100]
                                            plot (\x, {\x*\x - 4});
                                        \draw[dashed, red, thick] (-3,0) -- (3,0)
                                            node[right] {\small hits twice};
                                        \filldraw[red] (2,0) circle (3pt);
                                        \filldraw[red] (-2,0) circle (3pt);
                                        \node[right] at (1.5,4) {\small $g(x)=x^2-4$};
                                    \end{tikzpicture}
                                </latex-image>
                            </image>
                        </figure>
                        <p>
                            The horizontal line at <m>y = 0</m> crosses the graph at the two points <m>(-2, 0)</m> and <m>(2, 0)</m>. This means that both <m>x = -2</m> and <m>x = 2</m> give the same output, so the function is many-to-one. An inverse function does not exist over all real numbers.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-cubic">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the equation of the inverse for the function <m>f(x) = x^3 + 2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Write the function using <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> variables.
            </p>
            <p><md>y = x^3 + 2</md></p>
            <p>
                Interchange the input and output variables to begin finding the inverse.
            </p>
            <p><md>x = y^3 + 2</md></p>
            <p>
                Subtract <m>2</m> from both sides and then take the cube root of both sides to isolate <m>y</m>.
            </p>
            <p><md>
                <mrow>x - 2 \amp = y^3</mrow>
                <mrow>y \amp = \sqrt[3]{x - 2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Because <m>f(x) = x^3 + 2</m> is a one-to-one function, its inverse is also a function and we write
            </p>
            <p><md>f^{-1}(x) = \sqrt[3]{x - 2}</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-odd-power">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the inverse function of <m>f(x) = 4x^5 + 7</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            
            <p>
                Replace <m>f(x)</m> with <m>y</m> and then interchange <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>y \amp = 4x^5 + 7</mrow>
                    <mrow>x \amp = 4y^5 + 7</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Solve for <m>y</m> by isolating the term with <m>y</m> on one side, then taking the fifth root of both sides.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>x - 7 \amp = 4y^5</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{x - 7}{4} \amp = y^5</mrow>
                    <mrow>y \amp = \sqrt[5]{\frac{x - 7}{4}}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>f(x) = 4x^5 + 7</m> has an odd degree, it is one-to-one and the inverse function is
            </p>
            <p><md>f^{-1}(x) = \sqrt[5]{\frac{x - 7}{4}}</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-inverse-even-power">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine whether the function <m>h(x) = 2x^8 + 5</m> has an inverse function. If it does, find it. If it does not, suggest a restriction on the domain that would allow an inverse to exist, and then find the inverse for the restricted domain.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The degree of <m>h(x) = 2x^8 + 5</m> is <m>8</m>, which is an even number. This means the function takes the same output for both a positive and a negative input of the same size. For example, <m>h(1) = 2(1)^8 + 5 = 7</m> and <m>h(-1) = 2(-1)^8 + 5 = 7</m>. Because two different inputs give the same output, the function is many-to-one and does not have an inverse function over all real numbers.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-even-power-graph">
                <caption>Graph of <m>h(x) = 2x^8 + 5</m> showing that it fails the horizontal line test</caption>
                <image width="60%" xml:id="img-even-power-graph">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2,4) grid (2,12);
                            \draw[->, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[->, thick] (0,3) -- (0,12.5) node[above] {$y$};
                            \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2}
                                \draw (\x,4.05) -- (\x,3.95) node[below] {\small $\x$};
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue, &lt;->, domain=-1.35:1.35, samples=150]
                                plot (\x, {2*(\x)^8 + 5});
                            \draw[dashed, red, thick] (-2,7) -- (2,7)
                                node[right] {\small hits twice};
                            \node[right] at (1,10) {\small $h(x)=2x^8+5$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                If we restrict the domain to <m>x \geq 0</m>, the function becomes one-to-one because only non-negative inputs are allowed. We can then find the inverse for this restricted domain by following the four steps.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>y \amp = 2x^8 + 5</mrow>
                    <mrow>x \amp = 2y^8 + 5</mrow>
                    <mrow>x - 5 \amp = 2y^8</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{x - 5}{2} \amp = y^8</mrow>
                    <mrow>y \amp = \sqrt[8]{\frac{x - 5}{2}}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                For the restricted domain <m>x \geq 0</m>, the inverse function is
            </p>
            <p><md>h^{-1}(x) = \sqrt[8]{\frac{x - 5}{2}}</md></p>
        </solution>
    </example>


    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

